"Sonja Kisa" <marraskuu1978@yahoo.com> wrote:

> I enjoy playing with the VGA tiles. I find them more cute and
> user-friendly than the ascii version. But I understand why some prefer
> the abstraction of ascii.

> Anyway, I want to find a slightly "skinnier" version of the VGA tiles,
> so I don't have to scroll the window left-right all the time to see
> the whole dungeon.

> I know I can do this myself in Photoshop, if only I knew where to find
> a BMP of the default tile set.

> It would be cool if in the next version of NethackW, it automatically
> scrolled the window left or right, when you approach that side of the
> dungeon.

This version does; the default is not until you are within five grid
units of the edge, though, which I find doesn't give me enough "see
ahead", so my setting in my (win32 version) defaults.nh looks like
this:

OPTIONS=map_mode:tiles,scroll_margin:20

Once I have that working, I can see pretty well around me with just
a modest sized "peephole", since I'm always well centered in it, and
thus I find that I don't _need_ to see the whole map at once, and
since the 32x32 tiles are even more of a giggle, I downloaded them
from the official NetHack site using the directions there and added
this option, too:

# Use the optional 32x32 (large) tiles
OPTIONS=tile_file:tiles32.bmp, tile_width:32, tile_height:32

The only thing you'll notice aside from one wand that gets displayed
as a spellbook, is that many of the tile drawings missed the boundary
by a row or two of pixels, so something, usually a shadow, from another
tile is part of some tiles...oops!  But otherwise, the art is mostly
fabulous: the bats have wings that are translucent, e.g., and at least
one snake is real enough you are afraid to move next to it.

